A||N||8||LOWRIDEGT||My Exhaust Manifold Glows||||1||
Z||000000||LOWRIDEGT||12-17-1999||12:38 AM||||I did a HI-PO rebuild on my 2.8. The engine lacks power and the valvetrain is loud. It stumbles on acceleration but what is very strange is that the ex manifold glows like a christmas light after 5-10 min of driving. What is happening? Please Advise----  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||cobra427||12-17-1999||01:12 AM||||Hi, I had the same problem and found that it was a cracked EGR tube up by the plenum. Replaced it with a IRM flex-braid egr pipe and the problem went away. It is pricy ($99.00), but worth every penny. And you don't need that heat wrap around it either. Hope this helps. <P>cobra427||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-17-1999||07:33 AM||||It could also be a clogged cat or timing out of whack. With a newly rebuilt engine, I'd double check the timing very carefully, you have to jumper the ECM when you time it as per how the manual says. If the valve train is noisy, you also may need to adjust the rocker arms.||||reg||1
Z||000003||dogtired||12-17-1999||09:19 AM||||New rebuild V6, slightly out of time. Thats what caused my exhaust to GLOW. Timing can be off just a little and that will happen.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||02:56 PM||||Really good chili causes my exhaust to glow.  Don't feed your Fiero chili.  Just some advice from someone who knows.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88 Fiero||12-17-1999||03:46 PM||||batboy is right...check your timing it could be off....||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||12-17-1999||04:27 PM||||Noisy valvetrain: FIRST, make sure you have GOOD oil pressure!!! If something is wrong there you could ruin a "new" engine   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Next, what batboy said.<P>Hot exhaust (and bad acceleration): I'll vote for timing too   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Also check for any vacuum leaks (leave any hoses off, bad plenum gasket, EGR tube as mentioned, etc,...)<P>David.<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 12-17-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||06:53 PM||||You should be able to tell if the timing is off as far as yours would need to be by listening to the engine run.  It can't be very close if you are sluggish accelerating.  Please make sure that you follow the correct new-engine break in procedures... otherwise you have another very good way of killing a brand new engine.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000008||LOWRIDEGT||12-18-1999||02:40 AM||||Man ALive- If you guys ever have to do valve lash--Dont do what Haynes or Chiltons manuals say about 1 and 1/2 turn after reaching zero lash. It should only be 1/4 to 1/2 after achieving pushrod lash. I did what the book said and got burned BIG TIME!!What a mess-my car is at dealership after the guys who ported & polished my heads told me the proper info. I hope that I havent ruined my stainless valves and roller rockers and guides-I cant wait to see how much those UTI grads at the dealership want for adjusting lash--1000-100000 is a good guess--I'll keep my fellow fiero brotherhood posted on this-THANX- ||||reg||1
